Argh. And that one was very likely going to ascend, too...

We start our story on level one of the Dungeons of Doom, where an elvish
wizard pops into existance. 

*Pop*

@ - Hello world! Er, "dungeon"!
f - meow.

INT 20. Niiiice. 

Let's check inventory. Standouts:

? - blessed enchant armor (!), genocide (!!)
+ - charm monster
= - conflict

Planning to charm a lot of monsters, he names his kitten Alpha. On level
one, found on the floor, "h - a whistle. Apply what? (hoA) You produce a
strange whistling sound."

Awright. 

It amounts to almost too much fun playing through the first dozen levels
with charm monster at 0% and a magic whistle. Any time something got our
young hero in trouble, he simply Z-b-charmed it and he was out of danger.
An early store was cleaned out with the help of the ring of conflict. It
yielded numerous goodies: a tinning kit, a scroll of ID (price-IDed, too)
a bag of holding, and a stethoscope. 

The leitmotif of this wizard's life was luck. Mostly of the dumb variety.
When he needed /extra healing/ to work - with an 89% chance to fail - it
kicked in. When he accidentally stepped into (or in this case, onto) the
water on Home 1, he found out those snazzy +3 jungle boots were boots of
water walking. When he accidentally picked up a grey stone off a dwarf's 
corpse in the mines, it turned out to be a touchstone. When he forgot he 
was playing sokoban and started throwing force bolt, it didn't break any
boulders, thereby preserving his hard-earned luck. When he found himself
loaded down with unknown items, he started destroying the statues on the
Oracle level and discovered a spellbook of identify. (Not much later, he
found a spellbook of detect food, so he could practice up his divination.) 
Untrapping a dart trap on a lark gave him thirty-seven +2 darts, quickly
added to the arsenal.   

He was living the charmed life. The first few altars were coaligned. The
Mines were a breeze. His pets ate all in their path, the priest of Thoth 
gave out six points of divine protection, and Izchak's store had a magic 
lamp for sale, which, when #rubbed after the Mines, turned out to have a
grateful genie, which produced a wish. (When the careless human actually
playing the game fingerfehlered and wished for blessed +4 GDSM, our hero
got it. Of course, said careless human might have been better off to ask
for silver DSM, if he'd remembered that Wizards get an amulet as a Quest
Artifact, and therefore DSM was best used as a source of reflection, but
since that's not what killed him off in the end, it's a moot point.)

After finding a magic marker, he dipped his spare scrolls at the pool on
Home 1 and wrote enchant weapons/armors and some genocides. Liches? Dead.
Rust monsters, disenchanters? Dead. Eels and other ;-type monsters? Dead.
Nymphs and monkeys? Dead. With sprinkles. Mind flayers? Dead, dead, dead.
When he finished off Sokoban, after having found about eight BoHs around
the dungeon, the end level ended up to be the Amulet of Reflection level. 

He created himself a bag full of holy water, blessed what needed it, and
headed on down into the Quest. Where, now on pets Mu, Nu and Xi, he fell
through a trapdoor and landed on the Locate level. He went down two more
levels to find the Nemesis level. When he actually faced the Dark One in
the flesh, so to speak, the above-mentioned careless human remembered to
look up the Dark One's powers and realized he'd forgetten about curse-by-
touch attacks. (AKA the infamous "malignant aura.") He killed two of the 
everpresent vampire bats and dove for the altar. The first offer altered
the altar to the service of Anhur. The second granted Magicbane. (Stupid
luck strikes again.) The blade absorbed curses, he beat the Dark Lord to 
the stairs, and soon he had the Eye of Misspelling. While he was heading
back up, he tried to write "charging", forgetting that he hadn't seen it
yet, and promptly got M - a blessed scroll named ZELGO MER. 

He cleaned out a barracks around level 18 and got one very large pile of
random armor items, which brought his AC down around -15. Definitely not
ready-for-Hell level, but it should be good enough to survive the Castle,
especially with the Plan he'd cunningly devised, which went more or less
like this: 

Step 1) Enter Castle via backdoor, evading the traps via levitation.
Step 2) Tame dragons.
Step 3) Lead them into main area of Castle.
Step 4) Sit back and wait. 
Step 5) Use seven wishes from wand to fill out AK. 

While scouting out the level two levels above the Castle, he spotted a c
 - a yellow c - near the down staircase. He headed over to get rid of it
and then found out that the c had brought a friend. Or possibly his twin.
"Hiss!", they said. "I heard that! Crap!", I said. He was slowly turning
to stone. Fortunately, I knew the cure for that. Lizard corpse, one, for
the eating of. More fortunately, he had one with him. It tasted terrible,  
but it worked: he felt limber. Then a xan appeared out of nowhere. Stung
him right through his boots, injuring his legs and slowing him down. His
patience at an end, he resorted to throwing force bolts, thereby killing 
both of the cockatrices (as well as one of his pets), grabbed one of the
resulting corpses, wielded it, and stoned the xan (after a miss or three).
The above cunning plan was discarded, in favor of the following slightly-
less-cunning plan: 

Step 1: Scroll of earth for the moat.
Step 2: Force bolt for the drawbridge.
Step 3: Make statues. 

Blithely, he got out a scroll of earth left over from Sokoban and headed
for the downstairs. Still wielding the rubber chicken. Still hampered by
wounded legs. He stepped over the edge and into eternity.

"You fall down the stairs." (Oh crap, I didn't.) "Falling, you touch the 
cockatrice corpse." (Oh *crap*, I *did*.) "You turn to stone... --More--
Do you want your possessions identified?" 

Not really, no. I knew all of it anyway. The Bell, the +2 Magicbane, the
Eye of Whatever, the +3 Sting, 30 potions of holy water, enough food for
an army (mostly C- and K- rations), 20-some blank scrolls (or so), boots
of speed, GDSM, rings of regen and conflict, a bunch of spellbooks stuck
in a cache on Home 1, assorted wands, scrolls, gems, plus piles of smoky 
potions (restore ability, this game) awaiting blessing. 

And thirty-seven blessed +2 darts. 

I haven't looked for a bones file yet. Part of me really doesn't want to
know. The sheer poeticness of that character managing to kill himself in
defiance of such incredible luck - in his favor! - serves as the perfect
ending to his story. Having one of my future characters find a surprised-
looking statue on level 24 or whatever would ruin it.

Maybe, if it is there, I should upload it to Hearse...
